From 9fbc39d622dd44c66911c7732f15711f66e8f0bf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 15:13:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] cursor: rename only the tools that collide with a Cursor
 built-in
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Client tool names are namespaced on the wire because Cursor's harness
registers its own agent tools alongside them and Anthropic refuses a
request that declares one name twice. The refusal is not a clean error:
the run simply never produces a token, so the rename was applied to
every client tool rather than risk missing one.

A Claude Code client declares about thirty tools and four of them
collide — Read, Write, Glob and Grep. The other twenty-six were renamed
for nothing, and the model noticed. It could see both AskUserQuestion
and mcp_AskUserQuestion, was told in the system prompt that only the
prefixed copies were real, and concluded that instruction might be a
prompt injection. It spent turns reasoning about that in the open, told
the user "mcp_Write is genuinely in my available toolset alongside
Write", and wrote a page explaining how to work around its tools being
unavailable. Desktop clients got the rest of the damage: a question card
arrives named mcp_AskUserQuestion, which the client never declared, so
it cannot render the card and shows the raw call instead.

Rename the four that collide and leave the rest alone. The built-in
names come from asking a run that carries no client tools to enumerate
what it has. The match is case-insensitive, because the collision is
upstream's and "write" collides exactly as hard as "Write".
---
 internal/cursor/agent.go             |  7 +++-
 internal/cursor/agent_system_test.go | 62 +++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 internal/cursor/session.go           | 44 +++++++++++++++++++-
 3 files changed, 104 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

