From 1596ab64eb137b6438dd41aef93ecd4c46ef0ba2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 15:13:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] cursor: translate the wire alias back out of the model's
 prose
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The four tools that collide with a Cursor built-in are still registered
upstream under a prefixed alias, so the model knows one of them as
mcp_Write and reasons about it under that name: "I'll use mcp_Write to
create the page". The alias is this gateway's own invention. The client
declared Write, has no tool called mcp_Write, and neither does the user
reading the reply.

Translate this session's aliases back on the way out, in both the text
and the thinking stream. Only the aliases this session actually created
are rewritten — a blanket search for the prefix would also rewrite a
genuine mention of some other mcp_ tool, which a client may well have.

Deltas break wherever the upstream tokenizer broke them, so the rewriter
holds back a trailing fragment that could still grow into an alias
rather than emitting it raw, and releases it when the segment ends or a
tool call arrives to take its place.
